# Target C-II — a FREE superdiameter braid forces forest diameter topology and complementary hull cells

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; corollary of the square-root FREE tradeoff  
**Scope:** the `SQRT-SUPER-BRAID` outcome of a non-anchored all-inactive FREE reflection ancestry  
**Dependencies:** C65 full diameter visibility; C-WOODALL; sharp superdiameter-thrackle lemma; square-root FREE face/braid tradeoff  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_superbraid_forest_transversal_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The square-root FREE theorem produces, in its upper-rich outcome, a matching `M` of

\[
\left(\sqrt{3/8}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D
\]

old fully inactive source pairs with deficit below `0.49/D`. The sharp superdiameter theorem already says that the matching chords cross pairwise and that all their endpoints are vertices of the full source convex hull.

Those facts interact much more strongly with the actual diameter graph than was recorded in the square-root theorem. Every such inactive chord crosses **every actual diameter edge**. Consequently one inactive superdiameter chord already bipartitions the full diameter graph, so the canonical Woodall cyclic case is impossible. The whole actual diameter graph must be a caterpillar forest.

With the entire braid present, each forest component is localized much further: its two graph-bipartition classes lie in one complementary pair of boundary cells cut out by the braid endpoints on the full source hull.

---

## 1. Every superbraid endpoint lies outside the force hull

Let

\[
K=\operatorname{conv}(A)
\]

be the full source hull and

\[
F=\operatorname{conv}(B)
\]

be the force hull, where by C65 `B=V(H)` is the complete actual-diameter endpoint set.

Take a braid edge

\[
e=pq\in M.
\]

The sharp superdiameter theorem gives

\[
p,q\in V(K).
\tag{FT.1}
\]

But `p,q` are fully inactive, so in particular

\[
p,q\notin B.
\tag{FT.2}
\]

If, say, `p in F=conv(B)`, then `p` is a convex combination of source points different from `p`. This contradicts the fact that `p` is an extreme point of `K=conv(A)`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{p,q\notin F.}
\tag{FT.3}
\]

Thus every edge of the square-root superbraid is an external-to-external hull chord relative to the complete force hull.

---

## 2. Every braid edge crosses every actual diameter edge

Let

\[
h=ab\in E(H).
\]

Then

\[
|ab|=D>\sqrt{D^2-1}.
\]

For a braid edge `e=pq`,

\[
|pq|>D-{0.49\over D}>\sqrt{D^2-1}.
\]

The endpoints are disjoint because `p,q` are fully inactive while `a,b in B` are actual diameter endpoints. Apply the sharp superdiameter-thrackle lemma to the two source segments `pq` and `ab`. They must meet, and because they share no endpoint they cross properly.

Hence

\[
\boxed{e\text{ crosses every }h\in E(H)\text{ properly}.}
\tag{FT.4}
\]

In particular `e` intersects `F`, since every actual diameter segment is contained in the convex body `F`.

So the SQRT-SUPER-BRAID is not merely internally crossing. It is a complete geometric transversal family for the entire actual diameter graph.

---

## 3. One inactive superdiameter chord forces `H` to be bipartite

Actual diameter endpoints are vertices of the full source hull `K`. Fix one braid chord

\[
e=pq.
\]

The two endpoints `p,q` split the boundary of `K` into two open arcs

\[
I_e^+,
\qquad
I_e^-.
\]

For two hull vertices `a,b` different from `p,q`, their chord `ab` crosses `pq` properly if and only if `a,b` lie on opposite open arcs.

By (FT.4), every actual diameter edge has one endpoint in each arc. Thus

\[
B_+:=B\cap I_e^+,
\qquad
B_-:=B\cap I_e^-
\]

is a graph bipartition of `H`:

\[
\boxed{E(H)\subseteq B_+\times B_-.}
\tag{FT.5}
\]

Therefore `H` is bipartite.

Canonical C-WOODALL says the full actual diameter graph is either

1. one connected odd-unicyclic Woodall component, or
2. a disjoint union of caterpillar trees.

The first case is not bipartite. Hence

\[
\boxed{H\text{ is a caterpillar forest}.}
\tag{FT.6}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{d_H=q-h>0,}
\tag{FT.7}
\]

and `d_H` is exactly the number of diameter components.

This conclusion needs only one edge of `M`. Therefore in the cyclic Woodall topology `d_H=0`, the `SQRT-SUPER-BRAID` branch is impossible and every all-inactive FREE ancestry must take `SQRT-FACE`.

---

## 4. The full braid traps each forest component in a complementary pair of hull cells

Now use all chords

\[
M=\{e_1,\ldots,e_m\}.
\]

They form a vertex-disjoint pairwise-crossing matching on the convex hull `K`. Their `2m` endpoints divide `partial K` into `2m` open boundary cells. For a hull vertex `x` not in a braid endpoint, define its braid sign vector

\[
\sigma(x)=(\sigma_1(x),\ldots,\sigma_m(x))\in\{+,-\}^m,
\]

where `sigma_i(x)` records on which of the two open boundary arcs cut by `e_i` the point `x` lies.

The sign vector is constant on each braid boundary cell. For a pairwise-crossing hull matching, the `2m` cells have distinct sign vectors; traversing the hull toggles exactly the coordinate belonging to the braid endpoint just passed, and the pairwise-interlacing endpoint order prevents a proper subarc from containing both endpoints of every toggled coordinate.

Every actual diameter edge `xy` crosses every `e_i`, so

\[
\boxed{\sigma(y)=-\sigma(x).}
\tag{FT.8}
\]

Let `T` be one connected component of the forest `H`, with its unique graph bipartition

\[
V(T)=X\sqcup Y.
\]

Fix `x_0 in X`. Along each graph edge all `m` signs flip by (FT.8). Hence every even-length path preserves the complete sign vector and every odd-length path complements it. Since vertices in `X` are joined to `x_0` by even paths and vertices in `Y` by odd paths,

\[
\boxed{
\sigma(x)=\sigma(x_0)\quad(x\in X),
\qquad
\sigma(y)=-\sigma(x_0)\quad(y\in Y).
}
\tag{FT.9}
\]

Because braid boundary cells have distinct sign vectors, there is one cell `C_T` containing every vertex of `X` and one unique complementary cell `C_T^*` containing every vertex of `Y`:

\[
\boxed{
X\subset C_T,
\qquad
Y\subset C_T^*.
}
\tag{FT.10}
\]

Thus each diameter caterpillar component is squeezed into one complementary pair of gaps between consecutive inactive superbraid endpoints on the full source hull.

This is stronger than the bare forest conclusion. It gives a finite `2m`-cell localization of the complete actual-diameter endpoint set, with one complementary cell pair per connected component.

---

## 5. Crossing-incidence mass

C30 gives

\[
2q\ge\lceil D\rceil+2.
\]

Every loaded diameter endpoint has positive degree, so in the forest

\[
h\ge {q\over2}\ge {\lceil D\rceil+2\over4}.
\tag{FT.11}
\]

Every edge of `M` crosses every edge of `H`. Therefore the number of proper braid/diameter crossing incidences is at least

\[
|M|h
\ge
\left({\sqrt{3/8}\over4}-o(1)\right)D^{3/2}.
\tag{FT.12}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
{\sqrt{3/8}\over4}
=0.1530931089239486\ldots .
}
\tag{FT.13}
\]

This incidence count is not itself a contradiction, but it records how strongly the inactive shell is now interleaved with the force-visible diameter topology.

---

## 6. Consequence for the FREE frontier

The square-root FREE fork can now be written with the diameter topology exposed:

```text
all-inactive non-anchored FREE ancestry
    -> SQRT-FACE
       outer-face lower run of support span >= (0.604935...-o(1)) sqrt(D)
       + sqrt(D) near-unit inactive matching

    or

    -> SQRT-SUPER-BRAID
       >= (0.612372...-o(1)) sqrt(D) external hull-to-hull inactive chords
       deficit < 0.49/D
       pairwise crossing
       every chord crosses every actual diameter edge
       H is a caterpillar forest
       each forest component's two color classes occupy complementary braid cells.
```

In particular the canonical cyclic Woodall branch has no superbraid escape: there the FREE ancestry is forced into the long outer-face near-unit run.

The forest branch is now the only topology compatible with the superbraid. A closure there should use the complementary-cell localization against the existing forest component/outer-walk currencies rather than treating the inactive chords as an unrelated matching.

**Disposition:** converted the square-root superdiameter braid into an external force-hull transversal and an exact forest-topology certificate for the complete actual diameter graph; additionally localized every diameter-tree bipartition to one complementary pair of braid hull cells. C-II remains open.